    Part(
      text="""{
    "What Is Big Data and Spark?": "Big data refers to datasets that are too large or fast to process with traditional tools. Apache Spark is a distributed computing engine that excels at handling such data because it keeps data in memory, reduces disk I/O, and performs optimizations like predicate pushdown and efficient shuffle. Spark exposes data as read‑only structures called Resilient Distributed Datasets (RDDs) or DataFrames. In Spark, operations are divided into transformations (creating new RDDs) and actions (producing results or writing to storage). These characteristics make Spark a natural platform for iterative algorithms such as DEBISA.",
    "Why Instance Selection Matters": "Instance selection reduces a training set to a smaller, representative subset, lowering memory usage and training time while keeping predictive performance. For large, high‑dimensional data, the cost of evaluating every instance is prohibitive, so selecting a high‑quality subset is essential. Instance selection also helps mitigate class imbalance and removes redundant or noisy examples.",
    "Core Ideas Behind DEBISA": "DEBISA (Distributed Evolutionary BISA) extends the EBISA framework to Spark. It follows a multi‑generation evolutionary cycle where each generation is processed in parallel across cluster nodes. The algorithm avoids expensive sorting by retaining only the best parent and a few newly generated candidates per generation. By balancing the workload across cores, DEBISA mitigates data skew and network traffic, making it suitable for cloud or HPC environments. Its pseudo‑code is split into a preprocessing phase, a single‑EBISA module, and a full‑DEBISA wrapper that orchestrates the iterations.",
    "Setting Up a Spark Job for DEBISA": "The Spark implementation of DEBISA is split into three modules: (1) Preprocessing that loads raw data, cleans it, and partitions it; (2) Single EBISA that performs one evolutionary cycle (mutation, evaluation, selection) on a partition; and (3) a wrapper that runs these modules iteratively until convergence or a stopping criterion. The open‑source code is available on GitHub and can be run locally with recent Spark (e.g., Spark 3.x), Java 8+, and Scala or Python APIs. Users must configure the number of executors and cores to match the data size, and ensure that the cluster has enough memory to cache intermediate results.",
    "Assessing and Interpreting Results": "Experiments show that DEBISA achieves high‑quality instance subsets with reduction rates up to 50–90% while maintaining classification accuracy comparable to state‑of‑the‑art algorithms. Runtime scales sublinearly with dataset size, and DEBISA outperforms classical algorithms like MOCHC and EGA on large benchmarks such as Shuttle, Penbased, and Banana. Performance metrics typically reported are runtime, reduction rate, and classification accuracy (e.g., using nearest‑neighbor or logistic regression on the selected subset).",
    "Practical Tips and Common Pitfalls": "When using DEBISA, set the sampling rate low enough to avoid excessive overhead on small datasets but high enough to capture data diversity. Watch for unbalanced class distributions, as DEBISA’s selection strategy is less effective on streaming data or highly imbalanced sets. Ensure that the Spark cluster is properly sized; too few cores can cause data skew, while too many can lead to diminishing returns due to network contention. Finally, verify that the Spark version and the DEBISA code are compatible; recent releases are required for the provided examples."
}"""
