DEBUG    | 2026-09-10 13:20:53 | course_creator:run_adk_course_generation:113 | Event from ResearchAgent: model_version='openai/gpt-oss-20b' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text='Need to produce JSON with keys subtopics, notes summarizing research. Use search results.',
      thought=True
    ),
    Part(
      text='{"Instance Selection Basics":"Instance selection is a preprocessing step that reduces a large labeled dataset to a smaller, more manageable subset while preserving predictive performance. Core ideas include selecting representative instances, removing redundant or noisy points, and balancing class distribution. Evolutionary algorithms are often used to search the combinatorial space of possible subsets, encoding a candidate subset as a binary chromosome where 1 indicates selection. Fitness is measured by classification accuracy, subset size, or a trade‑off of both. Common simple baselines are random sampling, nearest‑neighbour heuristics, and clustering‑based representatives.","DebiSA Algorithm Overview":"DEBISA (Distributed EBISA) extends the EBISA evolutionary instance selection method to big data by distributing the population and fitness evaluation across a Spark cluster. The algorithm follows the EBISA flow: (1) initialize a population of binary chromosomes, (2) evaluate fitness on the full dataset using a nearest‑neighbour rule or proxy, (3) select the best parent and generate N‑1 offspring with crossover/mutation, (4) repeat until convergence or a target reduction rate. DEBISA’s key innovation is that each Spark executor handles a sub‑population, enabling parallel fitness computation and reducing the time per generation. Pseudocode for DEBISA is provided in the source code on GitHub (link omitted for brevity).","Spark Setup for DebiSA":"To run DEBISA on Spark 3.x you need a SparkSession with DataFrames or RDDs containing the labeled data. The dataset should be loaded into a DataFrame, with columns for features and a label column. Typical setup steps: 1) import the DEBISA library (available as an SBT/JAR or Maven dependency), 2) configure Spark with enough cores/ executors (DEBISA scales linearly with core count), 3) broadcast any small model components (e.g., distance metric) if needed, and 4) persist the input DataFrame to avoid repeated scans. Recent API changes in Spark 3.x affect the DataFrame reader (e.g., `spark.read.parquet` now accepts `mergeSchema=true`). No deep Spark internals are required for the user; the DEBISA module handles the distributed logic.","Executing DebiSA Pipeline":"The standard DEBISA pipeline consists of: 1) **Preprocessing** – load data, handle missing values, optionally normalize features, and split into training/test sets. 2) **Initialization** – create an initial population of binary masks (chromosomes). 3) **Fitness Evaluation** – broadcast the data, compute nearest‑neighbour classification error for each chromosome in parallel, and assign a fitness score. 4) **Selection & Variation** – choose the fittest chromosome, apply crossover/mutation to produce new offspring, and enforce a target reduction rate by fixing the proportion of zeros in each chromosome. 5) **Iteration** – repeat steps 3–4 until the desired number of generations or convergence. 6) **Output** – collect the best chromosome, interpret the selected indices, and optionally export the reduced dataset as a new DataFrame. Example commands: `val debiSA = new DebiSA(spark)` followed by `debiSA.run(dataDF, reductionRate=0.5, maxGen=50)`.","Interpreting Results & Tips":"The output of DEBISA is a binary vector indicating which original instances are kept. Key metrics to evaluate include: • **Reduction Rate** – proportion of instances removed. • **Classification Accuracy** – test accuracy of a simple classifier (e.g., 1‑NN) on the reduced set. • **Computation Time** – total Spark job duration. Typical observations: DEBISA often achieves reduction rates of 50‑75\u202f% with minimal loss in accuracy, and runs in hours on terabyte‑scale datasets when distributed across a 50‑node cluster. Tips: • Use a small number of cores per executor to avoid data skew. • Tune the population size: larger populations give better quality but increase time. • If accuracy drops sharply, consider relaxing the reduction rate or adding a post‑selection pruning step. No source material was found specifically for interpreting DEBISA outputs beyond the generic performance metrics listed above."}'
    ),
  ],
